WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 95, for June 8th, 2026.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.800
Starting the week with the latest from space and science, NASA will name the crew for Artemis 3,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.980
the mission that will test docking with commercial landers ahead of crude lunar landings.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.720
NASA will hold a briefing on June 9th at 11 a.m. eastern time to introduce the astronauts assigned to

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.700
the mission. Artemis 3 will use the third European service module to power Orion.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:33.700
The mission will demonstrate docking with commercial lunar landers in low earth orbit.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.180
This step directly supports the goal of returning humans to the moon's surface.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.520
Observers will watch for details on training timelines and land or integration tests.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.760
Now shifting focus to the hardware the crew will wear on the surface.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.660
The liquid cooling ventilation garment features a design described as very aesthetically pleasing

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:59.680
by project leads. It will be worn beneath the axiom space lunar space suit developed for NASA.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.780
The garment manages heat during surface operations on the moon.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.700
This marks the first public reveal of a finished element from the product partnership.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.140
Next steps include further integration testing with the full suit assembly.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.960
The company unveiled another completed component of the suit intended for NASA's Artemis program.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.040
The design incorporates materials and construction techniques developed with Prada.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.980
This element joins the cooling garment in moving from concept to hardware.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.840
The partnership aims to deliver a suit ready for crude lunar surface activity.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.820
Follow on work will focus on certification and mobility testing.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.880
On a different note astronomers have identified the source of repeating radio bursts.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.480
The source ASCAPJ1745 originates from a pair of stars in a tight binary orbit.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.320
Observations show the burst repeat on a regular cycle tied to the orbital period.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.120
The system provides a nearby laboratory for studying coherent radio emission.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.400
Data were collected with the Australian square kilometer array pathfinder.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.960
Continued monitoring will refine the orbital parameters and emission geometry.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:17.080
The ASCAPJ1745 binary offers the first clear periodic clock for a repeating radio burst source.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.340
Repeated observations tie the emission directly to the orbital motion of two compact stars.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:29.260
Because the system is relatively nearby, sensitive arrays can capture fine time structure

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.520
that distant fast radio burst sources wash out.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.780
The next observing season will test whether the burst strength varies with orbital phase

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.100
or remains constant.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.440
You know what is fascinating about this?

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:47.280
A pair of neutron stars orbiting each other every few hours can release more radio energy

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.540
in a single burst than the sun emits across its entire surface in a day.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:57.200
Picture writing alongside one of the stars as its magnetic field sweeps through the companions wind.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.320
The interaction twists and reconnects field lines in milliseconds,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.940
accelerating particles that then radiate in phase like a natural laser.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.640
The entire process repeats with clock-like precision because the orbit itself sets the timer.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.900
In the case of ASCAPJ1745, the burst arrive every 1.5 hours,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.120
matching the measured orbital period to within seconds.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.520
Yet no model fully explains why only certain binaries produce this coherent emission

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.340
while others remain silent.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.500
The missing piece is whether the radio waves require a minimum magnetic field strength,

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.840
a particular wind density, or an alignment we have not yet measured.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.380
Next up, engineers have tested a new spacecraft communication capability.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.940
The new system allows spacecraft to move between different communication networks without interruption.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.700
Engineers demonstrated the capability in recent ground trials.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.400
The technology addresses growing demand for flexible links as more commercial and government

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.020
assets operate in orbit.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.800
It builds on existing relay infrastructure to improve data return rates.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.480
Future missions will incorporate the switching protocol during initial flight tests.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.980
Shifting to business developments, a UK startup has raised new funding.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:19.320
New orbit space secured Series A funding to develop satellites for very low Earth orbit operations.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.740
The capital will support construction of the first demonstration spacecraft.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:29.880
VLEO platforms promise lower latency and higher resolution imaging than traditional orbits.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.780
The round reflects continued investor interest in specialized orbital regimes.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.440
The company plans an initial launch within the next two years.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.780
On the commercial technology front, another firm is preparing a smartphone demonstration.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.180
The Luxembourg firm will conduct a cellular spectrum demonstration in Germany next year.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:55.000
The trial uses an existing local telco network rather than dedicated satellite frequencies.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.600
Success would mark an early European entry into the direct-to-device market.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.800
The test targets standard smartphones without hardware modifications.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.140
Results will inform regulatory filings across additional countries.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.820
Meanwhile, ESA has formed a new partnership for Earth observation applications.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:19.080
The partnership will apply satellite data to development and impact finance projects.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:26.780
EBRD clients will gain access to imagery and analysis for monitoring infrastructure and environmental conditions.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:33.160
The agreement expands use of European Earth observation assets beyond traditional science programs.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.600
Initial projects will target regions where EBRD operates.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.980
Progress will be tracked through joint pilot studies over the next two years.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.220
In outreach news, Swiss Mint has issued a new collector item.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.820
The new coin honors Switzerland's contributions to space technology and missions.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.500
It features imagery tied to instruments and hardware developed in the country.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.660
Proceed support continued Swiss participation in European space programs.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.700
The minting run is limited and available through official channels.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.480
Collectors can expect delivery later this year.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:12.520
While those activities continue, ESA is preparing a major public event.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.580
The agency will occupy the space pavilion at the air show from June 10th to 14th under the theme

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:23.660
space for future. Exhibits will cover lunar exploration, climate monitoring,

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:30.560
navigation, and European launch vehicles. Visitors will see hardware models and data visualizations

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.080
from current missions. The event serves as a public outreach platform ahead of upcoming launches.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.820
Staff will conduct daily demonstrations and briefings.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:45.380
On the science application side, NASA has shared new findings from an archaeological site.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:51.320
Orbital imagery helped identify Jabal al-Faya as a former shallow sea location

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:56.300
now above the desert. Archaeologists are studying early human occupation layers

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:01.700
preserved at the site. The work combined satellite remote sensing with ground surveys,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:05.620
findings add detail to migration routes across the Arabian Peninsula.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.020
Additional field seasons are planned to expand the excavated area.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.800
While those seminars run, another European event is also opening its doors.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:20.540
ESA will hold a live briefing on June 9th at 5 p.m. central European summertime

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:25.140
to cover progress on the European service module and overall mission status.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.780
The update precedes the crew announcement by a few hours.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.300
Viewers can follow developments on the Orion spacecraft's path toward lunar operations.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:39.620
The session will include technical status reports from ESA and NASA teams.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.740
Recording will be available afterward for later viewing.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.280
Keep an eye on the results from next year's direct to smartphone trial in Germany.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.140
That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this,

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:56.120
a rating or review on Apple Podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:01.300
I'm Patrick and Vancouver. Thanks for exploring with me and I'll see you next time.

